e way the external memory model  works is that that  is the thing that actually costs  something  so the running time of your algorithm is  measured in terms of the number of block  transfers to and from external memory  that the algorithm makes  that's the external memory model and  one thing that people have known for a  long time is that the external memory  model really changes how you should  design your data structures  so a classic example of this is the b  tree  which is kind of the classical analog to  a binary search tree except for external  memory  and the thing that's special about a b  tree is that each node has a large fan  out so instead of fan out two you have  fan out order b and that's because  you want to make your node be the size  of a data block you want a node to be  something that you can read or write to  external memory in constant time  and the way that you maintain this  invariant that each node has fan out  theta b  is that you sometimes merge and split  siblings so whenever a node's fan out  becomes too large you split it and  whenever  nodes have fan outs that are too small  you merge them with siblings  and because you have this larger fan out  this fan out of order b instead of two  b trees are faster than their classical  counterparts  at least in the worst case  so b trees have the property that every  operation takes time  order log base b of n  instead of just log n  so of course it's tempting to just start  talking about dynamic optimality except  in terms of b trees  but one thing you'll notice right off  the bat is that b trees actually aren't  rotation based search trees at all  so in particular b trees do have the  ability to move elements up and down the  tree so that maybe some elements are  cheaper to access than others  but instead of the elements moving due  to rotations elements are moving  due to nodes splitting and merging  so for example if you have a tree like  this and you have  a key x  here  well you could spli