ming out of a node  has a buffer associated with it  a buffer that can store up to b over f  items  and the reason we choose b over f is  because well there's f buffers so this  means that within each node we're total  we're using a total of b space for our  buffers  and the point of these buffers  is that the job of a buffer is to  collect update operations that are  destined for the subtree  rooted at that buffer  so you collect update operations in your  buffer  so this is an example we just got  another update operation  and then whenever your buffer  fills up  when it's about to overflow that's when  you finally propagate those updates down  the tree  and this means that updates always  travel in batches so updates travel in  batches of size b over f  all together  and these updates now get placed into  the buffers in the node below  that's the idea behind buffer  propagation  and the result is that your queries now  actually take time  log base f of n because f is your fan  out so this could actually be larger  than what we had with b trees  but your updates get a huge boost  so  whenever we do work on updates we manage  to do work on  b over f updates at once and this has  the effect of decreasing the amortized  time per update by a factor of b over f  so you get a huge reduction  in time per update  so  one  kind of neat example of this to think  about is what happens if for example f  is square root b  so here we actually get the same  asymptotic time for queries that we had  with b trees  but updates actually take amortized time  a square root b factor than they did in  b trees  so this buffered propagation  technique what it means is that when  we're talking about external memory  search trees there are really two  different parameter regimes that we need  to consider  we need to consider on one hand  workloads that are dominated by queries  and for these workloads b trees are the  right thing to use which means that  the dynamic optimality results that  