a structures which we'll  call rotation based b trees  and then they were able to construct a  data structure that's within a log log n  factor of being dynamically optimal  against all rotation-based b trees  this is a really nice result because now  it puts internal memory and external  memory in the same arena as each other  now we're talking about rotation based  search trees in both settings  okay so is this the end of the story  and what i want to spend the rest of the  talk talking about is why actually  even this isn't the end of the story  so even rotation based b trees  aren't necessarily the right tree to be  looking at when you're talking about  dynamic optimality  and the reason for that is because b  trees themselves actually aren't always  the right way to do a search tree in  external memory  and that's because of an interesting  phenomenon that occurs in external  memory but that doesn't occur in  internal memory  in particular it turns out that in  external memory  there's an interesting asymmetry between  query operations so these are operations  that are looking something up within  your tree  and insert deletion or update operations  so these are the operations that are  modifying your search tree  so b trees have optimal query time  log base b of n  but it turns out it's actually possible  to make insertions deletions and updates  much faster than that  so let me show you why that is and just  to keep things simple i'm going to focus  on this talk on update operations so  these are operations that go and find  some key in your data structure and  modify the value that's being stored  with that key  and the way that you can make updates  faster than queries is to use a  technique known as buffer propagation  due to brothel and phagerberg in 2003  and the way this works is that you have  your you make it so your tree actually  has some fan out f that's actually  smaller than b  so now your nodes each have fan out f  instead of b  and each edge coming out of a node  has a buffer associated with it  a buffer that can store up to b over f  items  and the reason we choose b over f is  because well there's f buffers so this  means that within each node we're total  we're using a total of b space for our  buffers  and the point of these buffers  is that the job of a buffer is to  collect update operations that are  destined for the subtree  rooted at that buffer  so you collect update operations in your  buffer  so this is an example we just got  another update operation  and then whenever your buffer  fills up  when it's about to overflow that's when  you finally propagate those updates down  the tree  and this means that updates always  travel in batches so updates travel in  batches of size b over f  all together  and these updates now get placed into  the buffers in the node below  that's the idea behind buffer  propagation  and the result is that your queries now  actually take time  log base f of n because f is your fan  out so this could actually be larger  than what we had with b trees  but your updates get a huge boost  so  whenever we do work on updates we manage  to do work on  b over f updates at once and this has  the effect of decreasing the amortized  time per update by a factor of b over f  so you get a huge reduction  in time per update  so  one  kind of neat example of this to think  about is what happens if for example f  is square root b  so here we actually get the same  asymptotic time for queries that we had  with b trees  but updates actually take amortized time  a square root b factor than they did in  b trees  so this buffered propagation  technique what it means is that when  we're talking about external memory  search trees there are really two  different parameter regimes that we need  to consider  we need to consider on one hand  workloads that are dominated by queries  and for these workloads b trees are the  right thing to use which means that  the dynamic optimality results that  we've seen for rotation-based b trees  and for merge split-based b trees are  kind of the natural candidates these are  the results that we should be interested  in  but then the flip side is we also need  to consider update heavy workloads  so these are workloads where a large  amount of the cost that we're incurring  is actually from update operations  or you could also have insertion and  deletion operations  and here you want to use not standard b  trees but buffer propagated trees  which leads to the question  of what should dynamic optimality mean  in this update heavy setting  and that's the question that we tackle  in this paper  so the basic idea is that actually  a buffer propagated tree  has lots of opportunity to adapt to the  specific workload of operations that's  being performed  so where's the flexibility in this data  structure  well first of all different nodes can  have different fan outs so if you make  the fan out of a node larger what you're  doing is you're making it so that  queries that go through that node are  going to be faster because you're  decreasing the depth of the tree for  those queries  but  updates that go through that node are  going to be slower because  at that node they get batched into  smaller batches than they would have if  you had a smaller fan out  and so you can dynamically adapt how  friendly any given node is to updates  versus how friendly it is to queries  you can also it turns out carefully pick  the pivots in your tree in order to try  to split your workload in natural ways  so for example one thing you might try  to do is to pick a pivot  so that all of your updates go to one  subtree but all your queries go to a  different subtree  and that means that you could then  optimize those two subtrees differently  so that one is optimized for queries but  one is optimized for updates  and then finally you could change all of  this dynamically over time  so you could adapt to the way that your  workload changes over time  so what this means is that  buffer propagated trees  they're not b trees they're not rotation  rotation-based trees  but they are in a class of data  structures that has this ability to  adapt to the properties and especially  the locality properties of a workload  and what this class of data structures  is able to adapt to is the interplay  between update operations and queries  and so in workloads that are update  heavy  this is the class of data structures  that we should think about dynamic  optimality for  and so finally  we have an answer to our original  question how should we think about  dynamic optimality in external memory  well the answer of course is that it  depends on the setting  and in the update heavy setting the  question that we should be interested in  is does there exist a data structure  that is dynamically optimal  against all buffer propagated trees  and of course one of the things we do in  the paper is we formally define this  class of data structures so that this is  a question that's now well-defined  okay so  what i'm going to do in the last few  minutes of the talk  is i'm going to summarize the results  that we actually prove in the paper  addressing this question  so we do in the paper is we introduce a  new data structure that we call a jello  tree  and the first theorem that we prove i'm  just going to state it informally here  is that the jello tree  isn't dynamically optimal but it is  statically optimal  so what does that mean  that means that if you have a sequence  of operations that consists just of  query operations and update operations  the jello tree is dynamically optimal  against the class of static buffer  propagated trees  so it's constant competitive with buffer  propagated trees whose shape is fixed  these are trees that don't get to change  their structure over time  that's static optimality  so even this it turns out is a  non-trivial result  but then the next thing we consider is  we say okay well  maybe we can show some sort of continuum  between static optimality and dynamic  optimality  so what we aren't able to do is to argue  that  the gelatry is actually fully  dynamically optimal  in fact it probably isn't and it's a  really interesting open question whether  there even is  a dynamically optimal data structure in  this setting  but we can show that gel trees sit on  this interesting kind of horizon between  static and dynamic optimality  and in order to formalize that we define  the notion of a speed limited buffer  propagated tree  so we call a buffer propagated tree  k speed limited your k is a parameter  if the tree spends only a 1 over k  fraction  of its cost  on modifying the structure of the tree  modifying the shape of the tree  so a k limited  tree  isn't quite static it actually can  change its structure quite a lot over  time  but it's also not quite fully dynamic  because we're saying that it can only  use a small fraction a 1 over k fraction  of  its work of its cost  of its moving things to and from disk  it can only use a one over k fraction of  its cost to change the structure of the  tree  so the theorem that we prove is that the  jello tree is dynamically optimal  against the class of  b to the epsilon speed limited buffer  propagated trees  so here epsilon is a small positive  constant of our choice  and we're saying that if you consider  the class of buffer propagated trees but  you restrict them to be speed limited  then the jello tree actually is  dynamically optimal  so that's the main technical result in  the paper  and it turns out the gelatry ends up  being  a pretty  intricate structure both to define and  also to analyze and so one  open question is also just can we  replicate these results with a simpler  data structure is that possible  okay so the last thing i want to talk  about in this talk is i want to talk  about this notion of speed limitation a  little bit more  because i think it's an interesting way  to look at dynamic optimality  so this idea of speed limitation is  saying okay well  we want to argue that a data structure  maybe isn't fully dynamically optimal  but it's somewhere between statically  and dynamically optimal  and so there's two ways you can think of  it one is you can think of it as a form  of resource augmentation  so the way to think about that is we're  performing a competitive analysis  against an adversary which i'll call opt  and whenever you're performing a  competitive analysis you can  talk about this idea of augmenting a  resource so that you get more of it than  opt gets  so here what we're saying is you have  more ability to modify the tree than opt  does  so we modify  the task of modifying the tree costs k  times as much for opt  as it does for you  that's how you can think about speed  limitation as a form of resource  augmentation  another way to think about speed  limitation is as a restriction on the  inputs  on the sequence of operations being  performed  so in particular suppose we want to  consider inputs  who have the property that they're  that the optimal tree for that input  changes slowly over time  so this is essentially an input  whose where the properties of the input  are evolving slowly over time  well those would be precisely the inputs  for which a speed limited tree is  optimal  so one way to think about speed  limitation  is that you're proving  full dynamic optimality against the  subclass of inputs for whom the optimal  tree for that input evolves slowly over  time  so that's a little bit about speed  limitation  and now  having had a chance to present the main  results and also describe speed  limitation in a little bit more detail  i want to conclude the talk with two  open questions  so the first question  is just  how much further can you push this  concept of dynamic optimality against  buffered propagation trees  and in particular is there some data  structure is there some data structure  that even conjecturally is fully  dynamically optimal against all buffered  propagation trees  something analogous to the splay tree  that would be a really cool result  and then the second  question is  what would happen if we applied this  notion of speed limitation to binary  search trees in internal memory  so this would also give a continuum  between  static optimality and dynamic optimality  in internal memory  and what can we say about  dynamic optimality in that setting  so those are two questions that i'd be  really interested to see progress on  and that concludes the talk thank you  very much
 

